 [Input] About arXivLabs\nL148: # arXivLabs: experimental projects with community collaborators\nL149: \nL150: arXivLabs is a framework that allows collaborators to develop and share new arXiv features directly on our website.\nL151: \nL152: Both individuals and organizations that work with arXivLabs have embraced and accepted our values of openness, community, excellence, and user data privacy. arXiv is committed to these values and only works with partners that adhere to them.\nL153: Have an idea for a project that will add value for arXiv's community? cite44†Learn more about arXivLabs†info.arxiv.org .\nL154: \nL155: cite45†Which authors of this paper are endorsers? | Disable MathJax (cite46†What is MathJax?†info.arxiv.org )\nL156: \nL157: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, cite47†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , , and all contributors.\nL158: cite48†About†info.arxiv.org · cite49†Help†info.arxiv.org · cite50†Contact†info.arxiv.org · cite51†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org · cite52†Copyright†info.arxiv.org · cite53†Privacy†info.arxiv.org · cite54†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org · cite55†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org L159: \nL160: Major funding support from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - AMD-AGI/GEAK: Generating Efficient AI-Centric Kernels · GitHub (https://github.com/AMD-AGI/GEAK)\nciteturn28view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn24view2\",\"pattern\":\"v4\"}); Total lines: 463\nNo matching text found for \"v4\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ncannbot-skills:基于 CANN 生态的 Agent 技能模块项目 - AtomGit (https://gitcode.com/cann/cannbot-skills)\nciteturn28view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn24view3\",\"pattern\":\"2026\"}); Total lines: 220\nNo matching text found for \"2026\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nForgeStencil: Automating Per-Case Stencil Specialization from Kernels to 100+ Real Applications (https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06694v2)\nciteturn28view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn18view1\",\"pattern\":\"Table 1\"}); Total lines: 489\nL179: ### 4.3. How forging removes the generality tax\nL180: \nL181: The mechanism. The background argued that the generality tax is structural. The forged library makes that argument concrete. The levers the loop pulls are the standard repertoire for a bandwidth-bound kernel: register coarsening, vectorized loads, shared-memory tiling, and occupancy tuning. That repertoire is generic. What is specialized is the choice. The loop profiles a case, finds the binding bottleneck, and pulls only the levers that pay off for it.\nL182: Opposite decisions. Two bandwidth-bound kernels show what that means in practice. The same coarse diagnosis drives them to opposite decisions. star_1 (7-point) already runs near the DRAM roofline, so the loop pushes the only levers left: an fp16 register micro-tile, vectorized loads, maximal occupancy, and a launch geometry split in two. One variant serves cubes and one serves slabs. box_1 (27-point) is bandwidth-bound too, but it runs under register pressure, so the loop lowers occupancy on purpose.\nL183: It spends registers on a $j$-blocked micro-tile and leans on instruction-level parallelism instead. The same diagnosis leads to opposite occupancy decisions. A generic recipe must commit to one policy in advance, and it pays the tax on the kernel it guessed wrong. The loop instead forges for each kernel the strategy its bottleneck demands.\nL184: Real baselines. No generic baseline wins across the full matrix. The per-case winners are split among Halide, Devito, and EBISU. Table cite92†1 summarizes the 37 cases with a public-SOTA baseline; the complete per-case mapping is in the supplementary material.\nL185: \nL186: Table 1. Per-case public-SOTA baseline ownership across 37 cases.\nL187:  | Halide  | Devito  | EBISU\nL188: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL189: fp16 ($n{=}24$)  | 21  | 3  | 0\nL190: fp32 ($n{=}13$)  | 3  | 7  | 3\nL191: A shifting axis. In the next two cases the bottleneck itself moves, so the optimization axis changes. No retuning of a bandwidth-axis recipe reaches them. box_2 (125-point) is the library’s one compute-bound corner. There the lever shifts to cutting computation with a warp-level shfl reduction, and the loop also forged a tensor-core mapping, measured it, and rejected it. The diamond temporal-blocking family moves the axis the other way, to temporal depth. Several time steps fuse into one launch.\nL192: Intermediate time planes stay on-chip, so register and shared-memory reuse replaces per-step DRAM streaming. Measurement merged the 4- and 8-step variants into a single fused kernel, where blind per-configuration generation would have multiplied them. The same fusion idea extends to 13 kernels that fuse a stencil with the application’s physics into one launch. No lever is applied uniformly, and each appears only where a measurement justified it.\nL193: Beyond a fixed template. Numeric sweeps are peeled off into deterministic scripts, and the Kernel Agent instead synthesizes the concrete CUDA structure. An equal-compute ablation separates synthesis, search, and one-shot generation empirically: neither search nor one-shot generation reaches what the loop forges (§cite29†7.2.4 ).\nL194: ## 5. App Agent: Extending Specialization to Whole Applications\nL195: The App Agent carries Forge Engineering from the operator level to whole applications. For each real codebase it forges an optimization specialized to that application, validates it with the program’s own tests and timing, and integrates it. The central finding of this section is that, with few exceptions, these wins are per-application structural solutions. They fuse launches, remove host-device round-trips, fix a layout that defeats coalescing, or simplify an arithmetic formulation.\nL196: Drop-in replacement of a stencil kernel accounts for a small minority, and an unmodified generic-library operator for none of the 100 wins (§cite33†7.3.2 ). Specialization therefore has to be forged per application instead of built once and reused everywhere.\nL197: ### 5.1. States, milestones, and the Amdahl ceiling\nL198: \nL199: Figure 4. Application forging proceeds from baseline to framework optimization, obtaining the hot operator from the Kernel Agent, validation, and integration.\nL200: \nL201: An integration attempt has several honest outcomes besides success, and each should be recorded. The App Agent therefore tracks every candidate with an explicit state machine. A candidate starts in PENDING and leaves it only by reaching one of five terminal states:\nL202: \nL203:   * •\nL204: OUT_OF_SCOPE: no stencil-shaped hotspot, outside Forge Engineering’s premise.\nL205: \nL206:   * •\nL207: \nL208: BLOCKED: no fair baseline can be established, for example because the application’s own GPU baseline is unavailable.\nL209: \nL210:   * •\nL211: \nL212: RESEARCH: a real opportunity exists, but a validated integration is out of reach this pass.\nL213: \nL214:   * •\nL215: \nL216: DROPPED: forging and validation ran, but nothing cleared the gates.\nL217: \nL218:   * •\nL219: \nL220: INTEGRATED: a change passed the correctness and timing gates and was merged.\nL221: The non-success states are first-class outcomes. Recording them keeps the reported coverage honest.\nL222: The states are assigned at five milestones, so an integration can stop and be classified at a well-defined point. M1 locates the performance-critical region and establishes the application’s own GPU baseline. OUT_OF_SCOPE and BLOCKED exit here. M2a performs framework-level optimization. M2b invokes the Kernel Agent on the hot-operator case and takes back a validated kernel. M3 validates the change against the program’s built-in correctness check and timing. M4 integrates it.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nForgeStencil: Automating Per-Case Stencil Specialization from Kernels to 100+ Real Applications (https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06694v2)\nciteturn28view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn18view1\",\"pattern\":\"2.05\"}); Total lines: 489\nL295: Re-forging recovers additional headroom only where the new hardware moves a kernel off its binding constraint, for instance an occupancy retune on the one compute-bound operator. That is a config-level change on H100. On a B200 it is a structural rewrite. Once the memory-bound majority falls off the roofline, the same library transfers at 1.51$\\times$ across 41 cells. One rule covers both transitions. Re-forging pays exactly where the bottleneck moved.\nL296: The cost of capturing the gain depends on whether the freed resource is a configuration knob or demands a rewrite. The full round-by-round walkthrough, including the H100 box_2 decomposition and the B200 re-forge attempts, is in the supplementary material.\nL297: The map also generalizes beyond benchmarked shapes. The cubic and edge-shape geometric means are 4.11$\\times$ and 4.04$\\times$, respectively. Per-operator details are provided in the supplementary material.\nL298: ### 7.3. Application-Level Forging: The Specialization–Generality Trade-off\nL299: \nL300: Two findings make the specialization–generality trade-off concrete across 100 real applications. The observed speedups arise from per-app specialized solutions rather than from a uniformly faster generic operator. The realized gain also anti-correlates with the generic baseline’s quality.\nL301: #### 7.3.1. Speedup distribution\nL302: Across the 100 validated applications the end-to-end speedup has a median of 1.41$\\times$ and a geometric mean of 2.05$\\times$. Representative cases, including the outliers that lift the mean, are tabulated in the supplementary material. The distribution is broad: 11 applications below $1.05\\times$, 46 in $1.05$–$1.5\\times$, 22 in $1.5$–$3\\times$, 14 in $3$–$10\\times$, 7 at $\\geq 10\\times$ (Figure cite97†6 ). It also spans domains, with roughly $42\\%$ drawn from industrial codes.\nL303: Gains from a single generic accelerator would cluster. These span two orders of magnitude. The eleven applications below $1.05\\times$ cleared the harness’s no-regression floor, which requires $\\geq 0.98\\times$, and the program’s own correctness check. They remain in the count.\nL304: #### 7.3.2. The wins come from forging\nL305: Classifying the 100 wins by how the speedup was produced provides the most direct evidence for the paradigm (Figure cite97†6 ). Fifty of the hundred came from a forged, bespoke stencil kernel: 32 written for the application’s shape and precision, plus 18 fusing the stencil with the application’s physics into a single kernel. Another 33 came from a forged non-stencil kernel, and 17 from a pure host or structural rewrite with no new device kernel.\nL306: None came from dropping in an off-the-shelf generic-library operator unchanged. In practice, accelerating a real GPU application is rarely a matter of calling a faster generic stencil.\nL307: We separate two kinds of win. Some of the largest factors repair a naive upstream implementation, such as haccmk at $36.2\\times$ and bspline_vgh at $58.39\\times$. Their value is that the pipeline finds and fixes them automatically, at scale, and under an enforced gate, across all 116 candidates. The case for the paradigm does not rest on these outliers. The headline median 1.41$\\times$ is insensitive to them by construction.\nL308: On the 46 applications whose baseline was already hand-tuned, the forged solution still wins on all 46 (median 1.33$\\times$, geometric mean 1.59$\\times$, minimum $1.002\\times$). Each real code presents shapes and domains the operators were never tuned for, and no generic-library operator was reused. The end-to-end median is therefore itself a measurement of generalization.\nL309: Figure 6. End-to-end runtime for all 100 validated applications, colored by what was forged; below parity is a win (E2).\nL310: #### 7.3.3. The generality tax is measurable: speedup vs. baseline quality\nL311: The second finding explains the distribution’s shape. Realized speedup is inversely correlated with the quality of the application’s existing GPU baseline (Figure cite98†7 ). It recovers a large factor where upstream shipped a naive implementation, and lands near parity where upstream was already well tuned. This makes the generality tax measurable. The recoverable gain is set by that application’s own baseline rather than by any property of a shared operator. No single generic trick could capture it.\nL312: Figure 7. Speedup vs. baseline quality: operator-level Pearson $-0.60$ ($n{=}37$) and application-level Spearman $-0.70$ ($n{=}43$).\nL313: ### 7.4. The Measurement Protocol in Action\nL314: \nL315: The harness is credible only if it changes numbers we would otherwise report. We show it overturning our own results. The ablation then shows that removing any single requirement distorts a real measurement.\nL316: #### 7.4.1. The protocol retracts single-dataset cherry-picks\nL317: \nL318: The protocol is enforced in practice. Requiring the all-dataset geometric mean exposes both upward and downward single-dataset distortions (Table cite99†4 ). Each application’s headline field now stores that value.\nL435: ## Appendix C Representative Benchmark Cases\nL436: \nL437: Table cite140†7 lists representative applications with the lever behind each win. The large factors are launch-fusion and occupancy wins (e.g., bspline_vgh/QMCPACK at $58.39\\times$, haccmk/HACC at $36.2\\times$), not operator substitutions; pure stencil replacement sits at the modest end (fdtd_em/gprMax at $2.474\\times$). Values are the registry’s all-dataset geometric means where an application ships multiple datasets; only minisweep in this table does.\nL438: Table 7. Benchmark applications with win_source. Large speedups come from naive baselines and structural or launch-level rewrites, not from a faster generic stencil.\nL439: Application (registry name)  | win_source  | Speedup\nL440: --- | --- | ---\nL441: QMCPACK (bspline_vgh)  | launch-fusion  | 58.39$\\times$\nL442: HACC (haccmk)  | occupancy fix  | 36.2$\\times$\nL443: minisweep (minisweep)  | structural  | 5.775$\\times$\nL444: hypre (hypre)  | memory coalescing  | 3.857$\\times$\nL445: gprMax (fdtd_em)  | true stencil replacement  | 2.474$\\times$\nL446: FHd (fhd)  | structural  | 2.452$\\times$\nL447: QuantLib (bonds)  | structural (host-wrapper)  | 1.82$\\times$\nL448: RTM (rtm_iso)  | stencil injection  | 1.808$\\times$\nL449: HPCG (hpcg)  | matrix-free SYMGS (bandwidth)  | 1.712$\\times$\nL450: ## Appendix D Experiment Manifest\nL451: \nL452: Table cite141†8 maps each numbered experiment to the claim it supports and to the artifact it is measured from. The E-numbers are the ones cited throughout the paper.\nL453: Table 8. Experiment manifest: claim, support, and source.\nL454: #  | Supports  | Source / status\nL455: --- | --- | ---\nL456: E1  | app taxonomy / headline  | registry (median 1.41$\\times$ / geomean 2.05$\\times$)\nL457: E2  | app taxonomy  | registry win_source/decision\nL458: E3  | operator library  | robust_canonical.json\nL459: E4  | gates + operators  | revalidate_fp16_full.py (HEAD all PASS)\nL460: E5  | operator library (main defense)  | custom_stencil.py / stencil_kernel.cu (to compile)\nL461: E6  | gates + taxonomy  | 5 retraction cases (registry)\nL462: E7  | gates  | measurement-protocol ablation (5 rows, measured)\nL463: E8  | operator library (main paper’s autonomy discussion)  | perf_log.md (optional)\nL464: ## Appendix E Evidence Map\nL465: \nL466: Table cite142†9 states which gate validates which result. Kernel microbenchmarks and the application gate never endorse each other’s numbers.\nL467: Table 9. Which result is validated by which gate. The strict rule: no cross-endorsement between kernel microbenchmarks and the application gate.\nL468: Result  | Validated by  | Scope\nL469: --- | --- | ---\nL470: Operator-level forging  | Kernel microbenchmark  | A100/H100, per-case SOTA\nL471: Application-level forging  | Application gate  | 100 real apps\nL472: Speedup-source taxonomy  | Application gate  | 100 real apps\nL473: Measurement integrity  | Protocol (both levels)  | enforced at both gates\nL474: Experimental support, please cite143†view the build logs for errors. Generated by cite144†L A T E xml†math.nist.gov .\nL475: ## Instructions for reporting errors\nL476: \nL477: We are continuing to improve HTML versions of papers, and your feedback helps enhance accessibility and mobile support. To report errors in the HTML that will help us improve conversion and rendering, choose any of the methods listed below:\nL478: \nL479:   * Click the \"Report Issue\" () button, located in the page header.\nL480: \nL481: Tip: You can select the relevant text first, to include it in your report.\nL482: Our team has already identified cite145†the following issues†github.com . We appreciate your time reviewing and reporting rendering errors we may not have found yet. Your efforts will help us improve the HTML versions for all readers, because disability should not be a barrier to accessing research. Thank you for your continued support in championing open access for all.\nL483: Have a free development cycle? Help support accessibility at arXiv! Our collaborators at LaTeXML maintain a cite146†list of packages that need conversion†github.com , and welcome cite147†developer contributions†github.com .\nL484: \nL485: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, cite148†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , , and all co